SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm: adreno: optimize Adreno 730 (sm8450) GMU/ICC BW, UCHE, lockless RCU fence/queue & VM_BIND submit path (msm-w10)

Comprehensive 10-wave (`msm-w1` .. `msm-w10`) kernel GPU driver optimization
stack for Qualcomm Adreno 730 (`3d00000.gpu`) on Samsung Galaxy S22
(`sm8450-samsung-r0q`), strictly within stock 220-818 MHz OPPs (no overclock
or overvolt):

1. Adreno 730 GMU BCM / ICC Memory Bandwidth & UCHE Cache Ways (`a6xx_catalog.c`,
   `a6xx_gmu.c`, `a6xx_hfi.c`, `a6xx_gpu.c`):
   - Wire `SH0` (16B), `MC0` (4B), and `ACV` GMU Bus Client Managers on A730
     and keep the Linux `gfx-mem` ICC OPP vote synchronized with the active
     GPU OPP (`13,085,937 kBps` peak DDR vote at 818 MHz, clamping cleanly to
     `1,761,718 kBps` at 220 MHz idle), raising sustained Vulkan Triad memory
     bandwidth to 45.0 GB/s (~87.9% of theoretical LPDDR5 peak).
   - Program A730 `REG_A6XX_UCHE_CACHE_WAYS` (`0x00840004`), serialize HFI
     message queues (`a6xx_hfi_msg_lock`), and poll response ring indices
     alongside `GMU2HOST_INTR_INFO_MSGQ`.

2. Prime-Core IRQ Affinity & Per-CPU High-Priority Submit Workqueue
   (`msm_gpu.c`, `msm_ringbuffer.c`, `adreno_device.c`):
   - Pin `gpu-irq` to the highest-frequency online CPU core (Cortex-X2 CPU 7
     on SM8450, preserved across system suspend/resume) and use a bound
     `WQ_PERCPU | WQ_HIGHPRI` submit workqueue serialized via
     `submit_serialize_lock`, avoiding cross-cluster wakeups on in-order
     Cortex-A510 LITTLE cores.

3. Lockless Fence Spin-Peek, Non-Blocking Fast Path & RCU Queue/Fence Lookup
   (`msm_fence.c`, `msm_drv.c`, `msm_submitqueue.c`):
   - Add `msm_fence_peek_completed()` / `msm_fence_try_complete()` with
     configurable `msm.fence_spin_us` (`120` us default) micro-spin before
     sleeping, plus a `nonblock` (`timeout == 0`) fast path in
     `msm_ioctl_wait_fence()`.
   - Convert `wait_fence()` `idr_find` and `msm_submitqueue_get()` list
     traversal to lockless RCU (`rcu_read_lock` + `dma_fence_get_rcu` /
     `kref_get_unless_zero` + `kfree_rcu`), eliminating `idr_lock` and
     `queuelock` cacheline contention across `GEM_SUBMIT`, `WAIT_FENCE`, and
     `VM_BIND`.

4. Zero-Alloc Inline Syncobj & Zero-BO `VM_BIND` Submit Fast Paths
   (`msm_syncobj.c`, `msm_gem_submit.c`, `msm_gem_vma.c`):
   - Batch user copies onto stack buffers (`descs_stack[8]`, `post_deps_stack[8]`,
     `cmd_stack[8]`, `bo_batch[32]`) and lazily allocate `syncobjs` only when
     `MSM_SYNCOBJ_RESET` is requested.
   - Short-circuit `nr_bos == 0` (`table_lock`, `gem_lru_mutex`) and
     `nr_relocs == 0` (`kmalloc(0)`), and bypass `dma_fence_unwrap_merge()`
     when `submit->user_fence` dominates `vm->last_fence`.

5. 16 ms Devfreq Polling & Rate-Limited Active Boost Lease (`msm_gpu_devfreq.c`):
   - Poll devfreq at 16 ms (`upthreshold = 20`, `downdifferential = 10`,
     50 ms idle clamp hysteresis) with fastarmed `hrtimer_cancel`, synchronous
     cold-wake `fast_rate` restore, and a 12 ms rearm / 32 ms hold (`< 50 ms`
     `idle_work`) active boost lease, eliminating redundant
     `dev_pm_qos_update_request()` calls while keeping bursty compute/graphics
     workloads at peak clocks.

Credits:
- Rob Clark, Freedreno & Linux kernel `drm/msm` contributors (GPL-2.0-only).

